[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: What a plan node's retention is, and what collects it

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-PLAN-LIFECYCLE-OPEN
TITLE: What a plan node's retention is, and what collects it
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATUS: open
RETIRES_ON: >>>
Beads is running and one plan has reached the point where it would either
settle into durable specification or be collected -- the first real observation
of what a finished plan leaves behind.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
The current lean, held loosely: plans may not belong in main at all. They may be
working artifacts, or examples a model generates from, with only the durable
specification -- what was actually done -- committed.

Once beads exists a plan is a launch point, not a record. No plan survives
reality. Execution state lives in beads, which is not shared, and the plan is an
immutable snapshot taken at ingest.

An intermediate position, also unsettled: commit only the snapshot that led to
the work, for lineage, and never an in-progress plan. Then no plan changes
underneath anyone.

NARROWED. Plans are not structurally different from specifications -- same
nodes, same edges, same fingerprints, same machinery. The only open question is
RETENTION: how long a plan node lives, and what collects it. That is answerable
from one observation rather than from argument, and it is the same shape as the
question MECH-FP-PREMERGE-GC asks about what survives between commits on a
feature branch. One mechanism may serve both.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Recorded as open rather than decided because the author does not yet have a
sharp opinion, and the observation that would settle it does not exist yet. It
is the kind of decision that gets made badly if forced early and cheaply if
left until the evidence arrives.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
OPERATOR LEAN, 2026-08-27, recorded but NOT closing this decision: drop garbage
collection, use supersession, and make collection a separate feature after
milestone five. "feels nice and what I want but its bloating scope complexity
with garbage collection."

This does not retire the decision, and the reason is this node's own RETIRES_ON:
beads is not running and no plan has yet reached the point where it would settle
or be collected. Its RATIONALE says the decision "gets made badly if forced
early and cheaply if left until the evidence arrives", so choosing collection
now would force exactly what it warns against.

Two arguments worth keeping so they are not re-derived. Collection and
supersession are not alternatives: collection answers how the graph stops
growing, supersession answers how a reader learns X was replaced by Y, and
deleting destroys the second because the graph cannot query git while
DEC-SYSTEM-PURPOSE optimizes for a reader with no memory of yesterday. And
supersession does not foreclose collection -- a superseded node is precisely the
candidate set a collector would later operate on, which is a sharper signal than
age plus absence of inbound fingerprints.

Measured the same day: of 139 nodes, 64 are sketch and ZERO are superseded. A
collector aimed at retired nodes would collect nothing today; the growth
pressure is entirely at the ungroomed end. See DEC-SUPERSESSION-RULE.

Milestones three and four were floated for combination the same day and the
operator dropped it -- "just an observation". They stay separate: three is the
surface, four is the implementation.
<<<
